00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:18.580
Hello, and welcome back to another episode
of ItsDrDan, and this time we're going

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.640
to be going through electronegativity and
polarity, which can be one of the more

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:28.389
confusing concepts for a lot of students
when it comes to chemical bonding.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:32.960
So when you learned about this picture of
bonding, there's two major types of bonds.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.670
There's ionic, which was all about
giving and receiving electrons, and

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:41.909
then there was covalent bonding, which
was about the sharing of electrons.

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:45.569
However, one thing that you probably
found out in your chemistry class is

00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:50.840
that covalent isn't always this Equal
sharing of electrons that a lot of times

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.720
it's about this Sometimes it's not always
even if you've ever split something

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.109
with somebody that you might have liked
Maybe they took a few more or something.

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:02.880
Maybe it was even it's not always the
case So let's learn about this periodic

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:07.835
trend and we'll go through some examples
analogies of what this looks like so You

00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:12.335
might have seen the electronegativity
chart, something like this, where it's

00:01:12.345 --> 00:01:15.685
showing the periodic table and all of
a sudden there's all these different

00:01:15.705 --> 00:01:20.415
numbers and values, and what this is
reflecting is that different atoms

00:01:20.605 --> 00:01:25.514
tend to be a little more greedy when
it comes to electrons, and it has to do

00:01:25.514 --> 00:01:30.495
with their valence electron levels, so
something like fluorine, for example,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:36.930
That has 7 valence electrons, oxygen has
6, nitrogen has 5, carbon has 4 valence

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.280
electrons in that outermost layer.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.940
So fluorine, it really
wants to get that octet.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:46.539
It's willing to do whatever it
can to get that last electron.

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:52.960
And because of that, it's going to
lead to uneven sharing across covalent

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.554
bonds between Different atoms.

00:01:55.914 --> 00:02:03.645
So what we see here is in this trend
is that there is an unevenness towards

00:02:03.645 --> 00:02:06.164
the top right of the periodic table.

00:02:06.675 --> 00:02:13.835
So electro negativity is going up into
the right, and this trend is following

00:02:13.835 --> 00:02:16.565
the same thing that ionization energy.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.640
Meaning that these electrons,
these atoms are not willing

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.910
to give up their electrons.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.460
In fact, they're, they're on
the border of becoming ionic is

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.230
how much this uneven sharing is.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.590
So let's...

00:02:30.005 --> 00:02:33.735
Look, let's look at that and try
to understand this trend and see

00:02:33.735 --> 00:02:35.365
if we can, how will it be applied.

00:02:35.875 --> 00:02:37.855
So, most of this has to do with bonds.

00:02:38.095 --> 00:02:40.285
In fact, polar covalent bonds.

00:02:40.775 --> 00:02:46.575
And what this looks like is, it's
very similar to a tug of war, or

00:02:46.575 --> 00:02:50.165
almost like you're on a seesaw
between two different atoms.

00:02:50.825 --> 00:02:54.345
So I have a few little images here just to
kind of illustrate what this looks like.

00:02:54.845 --> 00:02:57.345
So the first one is
between carbon and carbon.

00:02:57.685 --> 00:02:58.915
the same atoms.

00:02:59.215 --> 00:03:00.725
So what would that look like?

00:03:00.755 --> 00:03:03.734
Well, being that they are the
same atom, they're going to have

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:08.294
the same amount of pull on each
other across the covalent bond.

00:03:08.524 --> 00:03:11.094
And we can prove this with
electronegativity values.

00:03:11.445 --> 00:03:17.185
So if I look at this chart, we can see
that we have for carbon a value of 2.

00:03:17.325 --> 00:03:18.265
5.

00:03:18.795 --> 00:03:20.845
So what can we do with that, with that?

00:03:20.845 --> 00:03:23.345
Well, we can try to
figure out the difference.

00:03:23.835 --> 00:03:25.195
So if 2.

00:03:25.195 --> 00:03:26.469
5 is for one carbon, 2.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.299
5 is for the other.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.010
Well, what is the difference between them?

00:03:30.369 --> 00:03:34.160
Well, we can do some little simple math
where we take the bigger value, even

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.359
though they're the same, subtracted
by the smaller one, and we see that,

00:03:38.429 --> 00:03:41.480
oh, when we subtract them, it's zero.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.829
So what that tells you is
that we can go to a chart.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:50.200
And see that, oh, there are labels for
what that difference is between them.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:57.179
So being that this is less than 0.5,
this is a nonpolar covalent bond.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.669
Meaning that it's an even sharing.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.310
So it's something that's, you
don't see more electrons going

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.619
towards one side versus the other.

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:10.350
So what does that look
like in terms of orbitals?

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:15.670
Well, if I have these two carbons that are
bound to each other, and I draw a little

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:23.560
electron cloud around them, it's going
to be a nice, even, type of distribution

00:04:24.129 --> 00:04:26.569
of electrons across that cloud.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:27.880
So it's nice and happy.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.030
It's nice and nonpolar.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:32.950
So this is exactly what these
different species sort of want.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:35.100
They want to try to be
nonpolar if possible.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.330
So, and that's what we're seeing here.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.749
Now, what about carbon and oxygen?

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.349
Well, we know that the value It's 2.5.

00:04:44.039 --> 00:04:45.599
Now what about oxygen?

00:04:45.609 --> 00:04:48.409
Well, once again, you can probably
take a guess based on the image Is

00:04:48.409 --> 00:04:52.039
that the the girl on the right is kind
of taking more of the milk electron

00:04:52.039 --> 00:04:54.109
milkshake than the guy on the left?

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.159
And that's because the trend is
it's going up and to the right so,

00:04:59.159 --> 00:05:03.090
you know oxygen is more polar So 3.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:10.860
5 Which is a lot higher, so what we
can show for ourselves to prove it, is

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.950
we take the bigger value, which is 3.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:17.449
5, and subtract it by
the smaller value, so 2.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:21.960
5, and what we will get
is a difference of 1.

00:05:22.069 --> 00:05:22.419
0.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.860
So, What that tells you is like, all
right, if we look at our chart again, is

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:34.479
that falls right into the polar covalent
area, meaning it's uneven and most of

00:05:34.479 --> 00:05:36.940
it is going towards oxygen in this case.

00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:43.230
So, whenever we have something that
is polar covalent, the way that I

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:48.745
like to always use the analogy is if
you've ever gone to Out probably with

00:05:48.745 --> 00:05:52.594
a significant other or friend and you
ever decided to maybe split fries or a

00:05:52.594 --> 00:05:57.445
dessert or something Probably a lot of
the times like maybe you might have had

00:05:57.445 --> 00:06:00.635
it even where you're each like cutting
it down the middle You're sharing it

00:06:00.804 --> 00:06:04.564
and that's really great However, some
people might want a few more fries than

00:06:04.565 --> 00:06:07.775
the other one when they're not looking
and then all of a sudden It's uneven.

00:06:08.165 --> 00:06:11.975
So and that's just how life
can be sometimes So with that

00:06:11.975 --> 00:06:13.835
we control that draw that too.

00:06:13.875 --> 00:06:19.545
So if I have carbon And oxygen, well,
this means that the cloud is going

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:25.895
to be more on Oxygen, because that
has a higher electronegativity value.

00:06:26.465 --> 00:06:31.545
So when we show that, it's gonna be more
like, little, little bit more teardrop

00:06:31.555 --> 00:06:36.175
shaped, and more of the electrons,
or the shading, which represents

00:06:36.175 --> 00:06:41.164
the electrons, shading, electrons.

00:06:41.175 --> 00:06:46.385
So it's gonna be a little more
towards oxygen, in this case.

00:06:46.825 --> 00:06:49.125
So it's gonna be more heavily favored.

00:06:49.755 --> 00:06:53.330
Now, With fluorine, the last example,
it's just like if you're sharing with

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:56.440
a little kid and they're taking the
milkshake, they're obviously going

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:00.819
to want all of it, so you're going
to see a very uneven side of this.

00:07:01.319 --> 00:07:05.449
So fluorine has the highest
value possible, which is a 4.

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:05.679
0.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:08.879
Carbon has, once again, has a 2.

00:07:08.879 --> 00:07:09.829
5.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.510
So what we're going to do is we're
going to subtract those two values.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:14.719
So 4.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.819
0 minus 2.

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:16.839
5.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:18.830
And that's a 1.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:19.760
5 difference.

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:24.810
So it's really, really polar
and in favor of fluorine.

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:29.840
So when we have that, how that's going
to look, it's going to look very similar

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.700
to the carbon oxygen picture, but another
way to illustrate that for everybody

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:39.520
that might be struggling to understand
this, it's very similar to a seesaw or

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:46.195
a teeter totter, where if I have carbon
on one side, and fluorine on the other,

00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:52.505
and this is my electron balance, well,
we're going to have more of the electrons

00:07:52.585 --> 00:07:55.155
on the fluorine side in this case.

00:07:55.505 --> 00:07:58.284
So it's going to be very, very uneven.

00:07:58.735 --> 00:08:02.135
So, and what we can do with that
is there's ways to label this and

00:08:02.135 --> 00:08:03.334
understand it a little bit further.

00:08:03.815 --> 00:08:08.315
So this picture is very much like
this one that I have here where

00:08:08.315 --> 00:08:10.235
I have a chlorine who has a 3.

00:08:10.235 --> 00:08:11.455
0 value.

00:08:11.724 --> 00:08:14.815
You know it's more up and right to
the periodic table as well, so it

00:08:14.815 --> 00:08:16.604
goes on the trend versus hydrogen.

00:08:16.885 --> 00:08:20.834
It's pulling or tugging all
of those electrons towards it.

00:08:21.124 --> 00:08:24.474
So as a result, it's something
where it's very, very uneven.

00:08:24.474 --> 00:08:28.224
Hydrogen just wants to share its
electron, but it's like, here you can

00:08:28.224 --> 00:08:30.075
have it, but it's pulling it away.

00:08:30.075 --> 00:08:33.265
So it's making it extremely
polar as a result.

00:08:33.954 --> 00:08:37.605
So, how do we illustrate this on bonds?

00:08:38.005 --> 00:08:42.914
So if I use, let's say, the
picture above, the HCl, how can we

00:08:42.924 --> 00:08:44.974
show that for hydrochloric acid?

00:08:45.545 --> 00:08:49.985
So with this one, what we're going to
be using is a couple different symbols.

00:08:50.415 --> 00:08:56.224
The first one is delta symbols, which
look like, almost like little music notes.

00:08:56.645 --> 00:09:01.255
So with this, so this is a
delta, So Greek letter delta

00:09:01.525 --> 00:09:04.625
and this shows partial charges.

00:09:05.405 --> 00:09:10.075
So keep in mind this
is not an ionic charge.

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:17.895
So for this it's very similar to
like magnets where there's a little

00:09:17.895 --> 00:09:20.155
bit of attraction between magnets.

00:09:20.344 --> 00:09:21.765
But you can still pull them apart.

00:09:22.105 --> 00:09:24.865
So with this one, the way
that we would label it is we

00:09:24.865 --> 00:09:26.885
would look at the two values.

00:09:27.265 --> 00:09:28.935
So chlorine was a 3.

00:09:28.935 --> 00:09:30.025
0, hydrogen is a 2.

00:09:30.055 --> 00:09:30.525
1.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:37.450
So, we can label chlorine as a
delta negative and hydrogen as a

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:43.600
delta plus, telling you that the
charge is slightly more on chlorine.

00:09:43.609 --> 00:09:46.070
It's uneven, okay?

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:52.490
Another way for you to show this
as well is using arrow notation.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.580
So, let's say if I take HF, for example.

00:09:57.285 --> 00:10:01.025
So fluorine earlier had a value of 4.

00:10:02.744 --> 00:10:04.854
0, hydrogen has a value of 2.

00:10:04.935 --> 00:10:05.484
1.

00:10:06.165 --> 00:10:10.954
So for this one, there's another
notation which uses arrow notation.

00:10:11.285 --> 00:10:16.814
So what this arrow represents is
something referred to as a dipole.

00:10:17.614 --> 00:10:25.744
So all these differences in charge,
so the difference in partial charge

00:10:29.199 --> 00:10:30.459
is a dipole.

00:10:30.579 --> 00:10:34.820
Okay, so that's what that
refers to, or a dipole moment.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:42.390
So whenever a molecule has a dipole
moment, this creates polarity.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:50.040
So the way we can show this, we can
use the deltas from before, or We

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:54.870
can use a plus on the hydrogen, but
remember this is a partial positive.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:11:00.030
So we then create an arrow
pointing towards the fluorine.

00:11:00.539 --> 00:11:05.220
So this tells you that, okay, hydrogen has
a little less electrons, but it's getting

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:09.710
Pulled towards the fluorine, and that's
why the arrow is going towards that.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:19.050
Okay, so this allows us to be able
to illustrate polar bonds by creating

00:11:19.189 --> 00:11:28.685
permanent dipoles, which is going
to be a very important concept For

00:11:29.205 --> 00:11:34.745
the future, when you start looking
at intermolecular forces and trying

00:11:34.745 --> 00:11:37.064
to determine polar molecules.

00:11:37.735 --> 00:11:41.074
Okay, so, now the biggest important
thing to keep in mind is, is

00:11:41.074 --> 00:11:42.464
this how you illustrate them?

00:11:42.974 --> 00:11:45.645
But you're going to be going
off your periodic trend.

00:11:45.885 --> 00:11:50.475
So remember, the farther you go up and
to the right of that periodic table,

00:11:50.935 --> 00:11:54.385
the more electronegative an atom is.

00:11:54.865 --> 00:11:57.165
So that's how you can kind of
tell if something is polar.

00:11:57.285 --> 00:11:58.445
The closer is the fluorine.

00:11:59.109 --> 00:12:01.790
And the farther, the farther
they are away from each other,

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.829
the more polar they will be.

